In all the reproofreading discussion, something is missing: in some
media (articles to be printed in Physical Review Letters, for
example), the stated policy of the publisher (The American Physical
Society, in my example) is that in the interest of rapidly
disseminating the results reported therein, ``proofs are not normally
sent to authors.''  In this case, the accuracy with which a typescript
or compuscript is translated to the printed page is of the utmost
importance.

In fact, The Physical Review has done an admirable job with their
electronic submissions program (kudos to Chris Hamlin). Now I only
wish that they'd support their normal typefaces (some variant of
Times) for REVTeX submissions rather than setting their journal in two
different fonts.

---Jon

